<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document>
<name>Road to War: five places, one story</name>
<description><![CDATA[Five areas of the South African War in the order the events happened. The gold line links them.]]></description>
<Style id="route"><LineStyle><color>ff2fd8ff</color><width>4</width></LineStyle></Style>
<Folder><name>Stops</name>
<Placemark><name>Area 1: Langlaagte, Witwatersrand: gold is discovered</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Langlaagte, Witwatersrand: gold is discovered</h3><p><i>1886</i></p><ul><li>The discovery of gold in the Witwatersrand resulted in British flooding into the Transvaal in a desperate attempt to gain control over the minerals.</li><li>The mineral revolution shifted the balance of power. The Transvaal was initially agricultural land, but the focus quickly shifted, turning it into an international imperial competition.</li><li>Britain didn’t want any other European power to gain influence over the mineral wealth first.</li><li>The competition for mineral wealth set the stage for conflict.</li></ul><p><b>Your task:</b> Explain TWO ways in which the discovery of gold changed the Transvaal.</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> Gold made the Transvaal a prize. Next, the newcomers who rushed in to mine it: the Uitlanders.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/1.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>27.99</longitude><latitude>-26.2</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>22000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>27.99,-26.2,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 1: Johannesburg: the influx of Uitlanders</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Johannesburg: the influx of Uitlanders</h3><p><i>Late 1880s to 1895</i></p><p>The number of Uitlanders in the ZAR kept increasing. What they wanted:</p><ul><li>Modern infrastructure, including better transport, water and sewage systems suitable for a capitalist economy.</li><li>An end to the Boer monopoly over the supply of dynamite and other mining equipment.</li><li>Political representation: they paid high taxes but had no vote.</li><li>The right to vote without having to wait 14 years.</li></ul><p>Cecil Rhodes, Prime Minister of the Cape, saw these grievances as a perfect opportunity to take over the ZAR, planning to use the complaints to justify it.</p><p><b>Your task:</b> Who were the Uitlanders, and why did their demands create tension with the ZAR government?</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> Rhodes planned to use these grievances. That plan became the Jameson Raid.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/1.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>28.0473</longitude><latitude>-26.2041</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>22000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>28.0473,-26.2041,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 1: Doornkop: the Jameson Raid</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Doornkop: the Jameson Raid</h3><p><i>29 December 1895 to 2 January 1896</i></p><ul><li>Pitsani, on the border of Bechuanaland, was the base of a force of mounted police under Dr Jameson.</li><li>Uitlanders smuggled guns into Johannesburg, preparing for an uprising, but then decided not to revolt.</li><li>Rhodes sent messages telling Jameson not to invade, but the telegraph line had been cut.</li><li>Jameson saw a chance to gain fame and ignored Rhodes’s order.</li><li>Kruger’s commandos easily captured the invaders at Doornkop.</li></ul><p>Aftermath: Rhodes felt humiliated and resigned as Prime Minister of the Cape. Britain’s fears of a Boer-German alliance grew when the German Kaiser sent Kruger a congratulatory telegram, and tensions rose further when the ZAR bought modern weapons from Germany. War broke out in October 1899.</p><p><b>Your task:</b> Explain how Cecil John Rhodes’s imperial ambitions were connected to the Jameson Raid.</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> War came in October 1899. By January 1900 the fighting had reached Natal, where the British tried to break through at Spion Kop.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/1.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>27.78333</longitude><latitude>-26.23278</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>22000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>27.78333,-26.23278,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 2: Spion Kop: capturing a hill is not holding it</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Spion Kop: capturing a hill is not holding it</h3><p><i>23 to 24 January 1900</i></p><ul><li>British troops climbed Spion Kop in the dark and the mist and occupied the summit in the early hours of 23 January 1900.</li><li>They did not fully realise how exposed they were. Higher ground nearby let Boer forces fire onto the summit.</li><li>The rocky ground made it hard to dig deep trenches, so the soldiers had only shallow cover.</li><li>Lieutenant Colonel Thorneycroft’s letter of 24 January describes the lack of water and ammunition and the heavy casualties.</li><li>The British withdrew and the Boers took control of the hill again.</li></ul><p><b>Your task:</b> Capturing a position and holding it are two different things. Explain why, using the terrain.</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> After setbacks like this, the main British army under Lord Roberts advanced into the Free State. In February it trapped General Cronjé at Paardeberg.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/2.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>29.52</longitude><latitude>-28.6</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>22000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>29.52,-28.6,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 3: Paardeberg: the surrender of General Cronjé</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Paardeberg: the surrender of General Cronjé</h3><p><i>18 to 27 February 1900</i></p><ul><li>General Piet Cronjé and his Boer troops were withdrawing after the British moved toward Kimberley.</li><li>British soldiers stopped Cronjé’s army near the Modder River in the Orange Free State.</li><li>Cronjé decided to defend his position rather than abandon his wagons and supplies.</li><li>The British surrounded his forces and attacked them. After days of fighting and bombardment, Cronjé surrendered to Lord Roberts on 27 February 1900, and many Boer soldiers were taken prisoner.</li><li>It was a significant British victory and showed how the terrain influenced military operations.</li></ul><p><b>Your task:</b> In which year did the Battle of Paardeberg take place? Name the Boer general who surrendered. State the results of the British victory.</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> With Cronjé beaten, the road to the Free State capital lay open. Lord Roberts’s army reached Bloemfontein on 13 March.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/3.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>25.07528</longitude><latitude>-28.99083</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>22000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>25.07528,-28.99083,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 4: Bloemfontein: the Old Presidency becomes British headquarters</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Bloemfontein: the Old Presidency becomes British headquarters</h3><p><i>13 March 1900</i></p><ul><li>British forces occupied Bloemfontein on 13 March 1900, after the fighting at Paardeberg, Poplar Grove and Driefontein.</li><li>The Old Presidency, built in the 1880s, had been the official residence of Orange Free State presidents.</li><li>Lord Roberts and his officers used it as their military headquarters, and it became known as Government House.</li><li>Why occupy the capital? For military strategy, for political control, and as a base to rest, gather supplies and prepare to advance on Pretoria. The army stayed for weeks, partly because of exhaustion and a typhoid epidemic.</li><li>The building became a museum in 1984.</li></ul><p><b>Your task:</b> How did the British occupation change the meaning and use of the Old Presidency in 1900?</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> After resting and resupplying, Roberts marched north. British troops entered Johannesburg on 31 May, the goldfield town from your first stop, and Pretoria fell on 5 June.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/4.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>26.2135</longitude><latitude>-29.119</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>26.2135,-29.119,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Area 5: Pretoria: the capital falls, but the war goes on</name><description><![CDATA[<h3>Pretoria: the capital falls, but the war goes on</h3><p><i>5 June 1900</i></p><ul><li>Pretoria, capital of the Transvaal (South African Republic), was occupied by British forces on 5 June 1900.</li><li>Photographs from the time show British artillery crossing the public square at the head of Church Street.</li><li>The Raadsaal on Church Square was linked to the government of the Transvaal Republic. Occupying it symbolised a change in control.</li><li>But capturing the capital did not end the war. Boer forces continued with guerrilla warfare until the Treaty of Vereeniging on 31 May 1902.</li><li>Think critically: a photograph shows only what the photographer chose to record. Whose victory is shown, and whose experience is missing?</li></ul><p><b>Your task:</b> Exit ticket: why is “Pretoria fell, so the war ended” an inaccurate statement?</p><p><b>How this connects:</b> Full circle: the British reached Pretoria through Johannesburg, the town built on the gold where this story began.</p>]]></description>
<Style><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/5.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
<LookAt><longitude>28.1881</longitude><latitude>-25.7461</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>45</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt>
<Point><coordinates>28.1881,-25.7461,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
